Let's use a channel to send data from a Goroutine and use it in [b]main[/b]().
Our program needs to calculate and output the sum of all even numbers in a given range plus the sum of their squares and output the result:
[b]output = evenSum+squareSum[/b]

We will use two Goroutines: one to calculate the evenSum, and the other to calculate the squareSum.
We will get the data using channels in [b]main[/b](), then calculate and output the final sum.[code format="go" ]func evenSum(from, to int, ch chan int) {
  result := 0
  for i:=from; i<=to; i++ {
    if i%2 == 0 {
      result += i
    }    
  }
  ch <- result
}
func squareSum(from, to int, ch chan int) {
  result := 0
  for i:=from; i<=to; i++ {
    if i%2 == 0 {
      result += i*i
    }    
  }
  ch <- result
} [/code]
As you can see, our functions send the result via channels.
Now we can call them as Goroutines in [b]main[/b]() and output the resulting sum:
[code format="go" codeId="3363"]evenCh := make(chan int)
sqCh := make(chan int)

go evenSum(0, 100, evenCh)
go squareSum(0, 100, sqCh)

fmt.Println(<-evenCh + <-sqCh) [/code]
We use the channels to get the result of each Goroutine and output their sum.
[note]If you do not need to send data to a channel anymore, you can close it using [b]close(ch)[/b], where [b]ch[/b] is the name of the channel. This is done in the sender.[/note]
